# The live conversation worker (C4/C5/C7/C8) — processes WhatsApp inbound and
# Salla orders every few seconds. Survives reboots (the nohup canary didn't).
[Unit]
Description=Career platform conversation worker loop
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target docker.service
OnFailure=career-alert@%n.service

# AUDIT 2026-08-06: this OnFailure line was UNREACHABLE, so the whole alerting
# path for the customer-facing worker was decoration. A unit only reaches
# `failed` under Restart=always when it exhausts the start rate limit, and the
# defaults are StartLimitIntervalSec=10s with StartLimitBurst=5 — against
# RestartSec=5 that allows at most two starts per window, so the limit can
# never be hit and the unit restarts forever, silently, however hopeless the
# cause. career-alert@.service's own comment described a state that could not
# occur: "a unit that exhausted its restarts and STAYED down".
#
# 20 starts per 10 minutes trips in roughly 100 seconds of a hard crash loop
# and cannot be reached by ordinary blips. The accepted cost is deliberate:
# once the limit trips systemd stops restarting, and recovery is manual
# (`systemctl reset-failed career-worker && systemctl start career-worker`).
# That is the right trade for what actually makes THIS process exit — an empty
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN, the single-instance lock held by a second copy, an
# import error after a bad deploy. Every one of them recurs identically on the
# next start, so «keep restarting quietly» buys nothing and costs the alert.
StartLimitIntervalSec=600
StartLimitBurst=20

[Service]
# THE WEDGE, and why this line is `notify` and not `simple`.
#
# The loop in run_worker_loop.py catches every exception and continues, which
# is the right shape for a process that must survive one bad message — and the
# reason nothing could see it die. A database that never comes back, a Meta
# token that expired, a poll that blocks forever: the process stays ALIVE, the
# unit stays ACTIVE, and nothing is processed. Twice, for real, with
# `systemctl` reporting active / Result=success / NRestarts=0 throughout and no
# alert anywhere:
#
#   2026-08-04 09:39:08 → 09:40:48   34 cycles failed against a Postgres that
#                                    was being restarted underneath the worker.
#   2026-08-06 06:58:04 → 15:59+     restarted onto code that reads
#                                    webhook_events.next_attempt_at, a column
#                                    staging does not have. Every cycle since
#                                    has failed on the same query: nine hours,
#                                    ten thousand tracebacks, not one WhatsApp
#                                    message processed, every screen green.
#
# Type=notify means systemd now hears from the loop itself: READY=1 once after
# the boot checks, then WATCHDOG=1 at the END of each cycle — after the work
# returned, never from a `finally` and never from the `except`. Both incidents
# above kept the loop LOOPING, so «the loop is running» is worth nothing and
# only «a cycle COMPLETED» is worth a heartbeat.
Type=notify
# Implicit under WatchdogSec=, stated because it depends on a detail of
# ExecStart below: /usr/bin/env EXECs the interpreter in the same PID rather
# than forking, so the Python process IS the main process systemd will accept
# notifications from. Wrap ExecStart in a shell and this silently stops working.
NotifyAccess=main
WorkingDirectory=/root/career
EnvironmentFile=/root/career/.env.staging
# EnvironmentFile beats Environment= — overrides ride inside ExecStart.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/env DB_HOST=127.0.0.1 DB_PORT=5433 REDIS_HOST=127.0.0.1 REDIS_PORT=6380 STORAGE_ROOT=/root/career/data /root/career/.venv/bin/python /root/career/scripts/run_worker_loop.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

# WatchdogSec — measured on this host, not rounded from a feeling.
#
#   the slowest cycle ever observed  18s  (2026-07-29T21:43:03→21:43:21, a
#                                         customer message with its LLM turn;
#                                         the other measured ones were 4s, 6s
#                                         and 10s, and an idle cycle is ~1s)
#   longest LEGITIMATE stretch with
#   no cycle able to complete       100s  (2026-08-04, Postgres restarted
#                                         under the worker and recovered)
#   every bounded network timeout
#   one cycle can chain             100s  (Graph 60s + Salla 10s + the admin
#                                         Telegram send 30s)
#
# 300s is three times the longest stretch in which a HEALTHY system could not
# complete a cycle. Tighter and an ordinary Postgres restart during a deploy
# would kill and page; looser and the deadline stops being a watchdog. Against
# the failure it exists for it is not close: nine hours becomes five minutes.
#
# What it deliberately does NOT wait for: the Anthropic SDK's own ceiling. Its
# default is a 600s timeout with 2 retries, so one hung call can legitimately
# occupy ~30 minutes — and a worker that has processed nothing for half an hour
# IS wedged, whatever the cause. The cycle's DB work is committed per event, so
# a kill re-drives at most the event in flight.
#
# REVISIT when the hourly sweep grows: enrichment calls Claude once per due
# customer, sequentially, inside a single cycle. Past roughly fifteen customers
# due in the same hour that one cycle can exceed 300s on merit, and the sweep
# wants its own timer rather than a bigger number here.
WatchdogSec=300
# Type=notify makes start-up lethal: no READY=1 within this window and systemd
# kills the worker at boot. The boot checks query Postgres and send a Telegram
# heartbeat before READY can be sent; a measured boot is 4s. 180s is room for a
# slow morning, not a licence to hang.
#
# What that budget is actually made of, corrected 2026-08-06 — the old note
# here said «30s client timeout» and understated it twice over. `requests`
# applies its timeout PER PHASE, so 30s is 30s to connect PLUS 30s to read, 60s
# worst case; and neither phase covers DNS resolution, which happens before the
# connect timer starts and is bounded only by the resolver. The Telegram
# heartbeat is therefore the largest and least bounded term in this budget, not
# the smallest.
#
# The database term used to be unbounded and is no longer: since 2026-08-06
# every engine in the process carries connect_timeout=10 (career.db.session,
# injected in the `do_connect` listener so the nine bare create_engine call
# sites get it too). Before that, `report_environment()` opened a session on a
# DSN with no connect timeout — a Postgres that accepted the TCP connection and
# never answered blocked there forever, which is the concrete way this unit
# reaches TimeoutStartSec and the reason SERVICE_RESULT=timeout has its own
# headline in the alert script.
TimeoutStartSec=180

# The wedge alert, and why it is HERE rather than left to OnFailure= above.
#
# A watchdog kill restarts the worker; it does not, by arithmetic, ever reach
# `failed`. One wedge-and-restart cycle costs WatchdogSec+RestartSec = 305s, and
# twenty of those cannot fit in the 600s start-limit window — so a permanently
# wedged worker would restart quietly every five minutes forever, which is the
# original incident wearing a watchdog. ExecStopPost= is the path that always
# runs: systemd hands it $SERVICE_RESULT, the script sends the operator a
# message when that value is `watchdog` or `timeout` — the two verdicts no unit
# can escalate on its own — and stays silent for the `systemctl restart` of a
# deploy. The leading `-` keeps a failed alert from becoming a failed unit.
#
# AND THE COST OF THAT, decided 2026-08-06. The same arithmetic that makes this
# hook necessary makes it a firehose: 86400/305 ≈ 283 restarts a day for a
# permanently wedged worker, every one of them reaching this script. 283
# identical Telegram messages is not an alert, it is a reason to mute the
# channel — and a muted channel takes the backup failure and the
# start-limit-hit down with it.
#
# The fix is NOT to retune StartLimitBurst/StartLimitIntervalSec so the wedge
# exhausts the start limit and the unit stays down. That was considered and
# rejected: Restart=always is the only unattended recovery this host has, the
# 2026-08-04 wedge healed by itself in 100s with nobody awake, and the same two
# knobs govern the hung-start verdict — so a Postgres merely slow to come back
# at boot would leave the worker permanently dead AFTER the database recovered.
# The start limit therefore stays deliberately unreachable, and that is
# asserted by tests/test_ops_watchdogs.py::TestTheAlertDoesNotBecomeAStorm so
# nobody retunes it without reading this.
#
# The storm is a property of the NOTIFICATION, so it is fixed there: the script
# sends one message when the failure starts, a reminder every REPEAT_S=3600
# carrying how long it has been broken and how many kills it stands for, and a
# distinct sentence when a failure RETURNS after a quiet spell instead of never
# having stopped. At most 24 messages a day, none of them identical.
ExecStopPost=-/root/career/scripts/alert_unit_failure.sh %n

# DEPLOY ORDER — three files MUST land before this one.
#
#   src/career/notify.py            the sd_notify writer
#   scripts/run_worker_loop.py      READY=1 and the per-cycle WATCHDOG=1
#   scripts/alert_unit_failure.sh   knows to stay silent unless the result is
#                                   `watchdog`; the old copy would page the
#                                   operator on every ordinary restart, this
#                                   deploy's own included
#
# Only then this file, then `systemctl daemon-reload`, then
# `systemctl restart career-worker`. Type=notify against a worker that cannot
# say READY=1 is a worker systemd kills at TimeoutStartSec and keeps killing.
# Proof it took: the journal line
# «systemd watchdog armed: a cycle must complete every 300s».

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
